<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Vectors, Geometry, and Equations in Space</title>

  <introduction>
   <p>
    This chapter introduces vectors as mathematical objects that describe both magnitude and direction.
    We begin in the plane, learning how to represent vectors using correct notation and how to combine
    them using basic operations such as addition, subtraction, and scalar multiplication. From there,
    we move to component form and unit vectors, which provide a flexible algebraic way to work with
    vectors in calculations and applications.
  </p>

  <p>
    We then extend these ideas into three-dimensional space, developing the geometric tools needed
    to describe points, distances, lines, and planes. Along the way, we introduce the dot product and
    cross product, highlighting how these operations connect algebraic computations to geometric ideas
    such as angles, projections, areas, and orientation in space.
  </p>

  <p>
    By the end of this chapter, you should be comfortable translating between geometric descriptions
    and algebraic expressions involving vectors, and using vector methods to analyze problems in
    two and three dimensions. These tools form the foundation for much of the work in the remainder
    of the course.
  </p>
  </introduction>

